Why this commit makes the app usable rather than just compilable.

The first native commit established the modules and a shell that could draw the
graph but not interact with it. A Git client that cannot show what a commit
changed or what an agent said is not doing its job, so this wires the
interactions that turn the shell into a working tool:

- Click handling on the Cairo-drawn graph maps the y position back to a commit
  row (the same row height the renderer uses) so selecting a commit is a direct
  hit test, not a separate list widget that would drift out of sync with the
  lanes.
- Selecting a commit loads its diff via the git module and renders it in a
  monospace text view; a 'Review this change' action sends the diff to the local
  AI backend on a worker thread and posts the result back through a GLib idle
  poll, keeping the UI thread responsive.
- Selecting a session loads its transcript from the read-only opencode database
  and offers 'Summarize this session', again off the UI thread.

The AI calls go through the same backend selection as before (Ollama preferred,
opencode service fallback) and run in a background thread because a local model
can take tens of seconds; blocking the GTK main loop would freeze the window.
